#plant-header("Skyline Honeylocust", "Gleditsia triacanthos 'Skycole'", "honeylocust", establishment: plant-established("honeylocust"), lifespan: plant-lifespan("honeylocust"), tier: plant-tier("honeylocust"), failure: plant-failure("honeylocust"))[
 Skyline Honeylocust is one of the most reliable shade trees for the Front Range. Its feathery compound foliage creates dappled shade that allows grass to thrive beneath it. Thornless and seedless, it tolerates drought, alkaline soils, heat, and air pollution well. Golden-yellow fall color is dramatic and long-lasting. Located in the center of the front yard lawn zone in full sun.
]

=== Watering

This tree is in the center of the front lawn zone and receives water from the Rachio sprinkler system — not the drip system. Rachio's ET/MAD weather intelligence is already enabled and handles weather-based adjustment automatically.

Unlike the fully established Silver Maples, this is a second-season plant with a root system that is not yet self-sufficient. Sprinkler coverage adequate for established trees and turf may not be sufficient for a young tree in full sun during July and August heat. Supplement with deep hand watering at the drip line during extended heat waves — a slow soak at the outer root zone, once or twice during prolonged heat. Signs of stress in young trees: wilting by mid-afternoon that does not recover overnight, leaf scorch at margins, or premature leaf drop.

Full sun exposure increases water demand relative to partially shaded trees. During establishment, err on the side of more water during July and August.

=== Fertilization

Follow the audited program: April liquid N-K (15-0-15), May chelated micronutrient blend, and EDDHA chelated iron drenches in May and June. Honeylocust is the highest chlorosis-risk tree in this landscape, so the EDDHA sequence is a core correction, not an optional add-on. Keep spring N moderate and avoid late-season N.

=== Pruning & Maintenance

Prune in late winter (February–March) while dormant. Maintain the natural vase shape; remove dead, damaged, or crossing branches. Train young trees to a central leader. Avoid heavy pruning — honeylocust is prone to decay in large wounds. Clean pruning tools with 10% bleach solution between cuts.

=== Wildlife Value

Skyline Honeylocust is a seedless variety, so it provides minimal wildlife food value. However, the open, dappled canopy provides nesting cover for collared doves and foraging perches for house finches and red-wing blackbirds. The bark and branches may attract northern flickers probing for insects. Cottontail rabbits occasionally browse young twigs in severe winter conditions. The light canopy allows for good grass growth beneath it, which benefits ground-feeding birds.

=== Pests & Disease

#link("https://www.google.com/search?q=honeylocust+plant+bug+treatment")[Honeylocust plant bug] causes speckled, distorted foliage in spring — treat with neem oil or pyrethroid spray when nymphs are active (May). #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=honeylocust+pod+gall+midge")[Pod gall midge] creates swollen distorted leaf tips; usually cosmetic only, no treatment required. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=honeylocust+spider+mites+treatment")[Spider mites] flare in hot, dry summers — look for stippled yellowing; treat with horticultural oil or bifenthrin. Iron #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=honeylocust+chlorosis+treatment+Colorado")[chlorosis] (yellowing leaves with green veins) is the most important ongoing concern — address with EDDHA chelated iron annually in May and again in June.

=== Other Care

Mulch 3 inches deep within the drip line, extended to the full drip line. Wrap young trunks in fall to prevent frost cracking on the southwest side — use paper-based tree wrap (not plastic) and remove in early March. Monitor for broken or hanging branches after summer storms, as this is one of the faster-growing trees in the landscape.
